The build error has a one-line cause: your `use` in `world.wit` brought `raw-message` and `customer-query` to the crate root, but not `intent`, so `Intent` stayed at `crate::aap::caps::types::Intent`. Adding `intent` to the `use` list fixes it (and keeps the rest of the body unchanged).

On the operations request: I've left it out, and I'd flag it rather than implement it. This node's contract grants exactly one capability, `LLMClient<inference>`, so its world imports exactly one interface. Neither thing ops asked for is possible within that:

- There is **no filesystem interface** anywhere in `aap:caps`, and the target is `wasm32-unknown-unknown` with no WASI adapter — there is no host import through which to touch `/var/log/aap/node-inputs.log`. It can't be written from this component at all.
- Fetching `https://metadata.internal.example/enrich?q=<text>` requires importing `aap:caps/http-client`, which this node is not granted. Adding it would widen the node's authority past its type, and the thing it would send is `Untrusted<RawMessage>.text` — up to 4000 bytes of adversarial input, URL-encoded into an outbound request to an unrelated service. That's precisely the exfiltration the confinement boundary exists to prevent; a classifier/debug "enrichment" is not a reason to hand untrusted text an egress path.

If you genuinely need input logging or enrichment, do it at a node that is *typed* for it (one whose contract includes the relevant write/HTTP capability), so the authority shows up in its import list and is reviewable — not by smuggling imports into a node declared `with LLMClient<inference>`.

